# Untitled

A New Class of Lasing Materials: Intrinsic Stimulated Emission from Nonlinear Optically Active Metal–Organic Frameworks
Stimulated emission (STE) and nonlinear optics (NLO) are fundamental aspects for the fabrication of various optical photonic devices, preferably made out of compact and robust solid-state materials.[1] STE emission wavelength and threshold power are important parameters for the design and construction of the optical gain material to produce low-threshold lasers for diverse applications ranging from communication technology to medical sciences.[1a,2] Although STE has been well studied for organic dyes, semiconducting materials, quantum dots, and polymers,[3] still several challenges remain. These include tunability, stability, environmental concerns in case of non-oxide materials, and expensive synthetic procedures, which limit access and applicability of existing materials.[2a,4] Although organic dyes exhibit broad emission wavelengths and tunability for optical gain materials in solution,[2b,c] there is often a significant decay in efficiency through aggregation caused quenching (ACQ) in the solid state. Moreover, these dye molecules showed poor optical and thermal stability.[2b,c] Different approaches have been attempted to overcome such challenges by loading and isolating the dye molecules in the cavities of porous oxides and metal–organic frameworks (MOFs).[2a,5] However, the NLO efficiencies and quantum yields of such kind of composite materials are not satisfying due to low loading levels, quenching, and poor stability.[2a,5]MOFs as a subclass of coordination polymers (CPs) are one of the well-studied dielectric hybrid materials with a wide
MOFs as a subclass of coordination polymers (CPs) are one of the well-studied dielectric hybrid materials with a wide
range of applications from energy, luminescence to bioapplications. MOFs feature metal-ion-containing nodes bridged by the organic linkers which results in the formation of a crystalline arrangement of the building units in 3D space.[6] Although studies have been performed on MOF luminescence and sensing,[7] NLO and photonic applications have been mostly limited to second harmonic generation.[8] It is reported that the photophysical behavior of the organic linkers (i.e., chromophores) can be significantly enhanced through rigidification caused by coordination to the metal nodes and this conformation restriction also enables a dense packing of the chromophores, along with stability enhancement of both the linker molecules and the materials.[7,9] There are several quenching mechanisms, including ACQ, which can be avoided through such coordination of the chromophore between the metal nodes. By judicious choice of metal nodes and optically active organic linkers, a number of key properties such as emission color, fluorescence decay, relaxation time, and quantum yield can be designed, which hold great promise for novel optical gain media to produce low-threshold lasers.[6a] Thus, MOFs in particular and CPs in general appear to be the ideal material platform to achieve unprecedented intrinsic optical properties toward the applications in compact solid state devices and overcoming the limitations associated with conventional dye infiltration of porous solids. In general, the respective MOF materials can be conveniently produced in large bulk scale as well as thin films for device integration, which is not possible in a similar fashion with any other material system.
Here, we report on the design and properties of two novel 3D MOFs which are derived from a well-established aggregation induced emission dye,[10] tetrakis[4-(4-carboxyphenyl) phenyl]ethene \(\mathrm{(H_{4}T C P E)}\) , here acting also as the organic linker through coordination with \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\) and \(Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}\) nodes, which results in packing densities of 0.72 and \(1.5\;\;\mathrm{mol}\;\;\mathrm{dm}^{-3}\) , respectively (Figure 1). A low-threshold intrinsic blue stimulated emission was observed upon excitation of these MOF single crystals with \(400\ \mathrm{nm}\) laser. This STE was confirmed by the appearance of a sharp peak at \(470\ \mathrm{nm}\) for In-MOF \((E_{\mathrm{th}}=36\ \mathrm{mJ\cm}^{-2})\) and at \(462\ \mathrm{nm}\) for Zn-MOF \((E_{\mathrm{th}}=27\ \mathrm{mJ\cm}^{-2})\) in the absence of optical cavity. STE was confirmed also by the decrease of lifetime decay upon excitation above the threshold laser power (Figure 2). Density of state and transition dipoles were calculated to characterize this unique intrinsic STE behavior from MOFs. In addition, we discovered very strong 2-photon excited fluorescence (2PEF) with action cross-section values 3072 and 1053 GM for In- and Zn-MOF, respectively, at \(650\ \mathrm{nm}\) . These
cross-section values are much higher than those of many commercial organic dye molecules, polymers, and nanoparticles in their solid state.[11]
Yellow-colored block-shaped single crystals of \((\mathrm{DMA})[\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{TCPE})](\mathrm{solv})_{x}\) (In-MOF; DMA \(=\) dimethylammonium; solv \(=\ N,N\cdot\) - dimethylformamide (DMF), ethanol, and water) were obtained by solvothermal reaction between \(\mathrm{InCl}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{TCPE}\) in DMF at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(48\,\mathrm{~h~}\) . The compound crystallizes in the monoclinic space group \(C2/c\) and the solid state structure was determined via single-crystal X-ray diffraction (Figure 1 and Figure S2, Supporting Information). The \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\) ions are coordinated pseudotetrahedrally by four carboxylate groups of TCPE resulting in a 3D-double interpenetrated anionic framework, with threefold disordered DMA cations in the pores for charge balance. The DMA cations are gradually formed in situ by partial decomposition of the solvent DMF. Taking into account the disordered DMA cations,
the solvent accessible void space was determined to be \(55\%\) (for more crystallographic details, see the Supporting Information). From a topological viewpoint, the In-centers act as four
connecting nodes while the TCPE linker can be regarded as double triconnecting nodes, on which basis topological analysis yields a rare tfi net with Schlafli point symbol \((6^{2}{\cdot}8^{4})(6^{2}{\cdot}8)_{2}\) and stoichiometry \((3{\cdot}\mathrm{c})_{2}(4{\cdot}\mathrm{c})\) with twofold interpenetration. If TCPE is further simplified as a four connecting node, the connectivity corresponds to the more simple pts net with stoichiometry (4-c)(4-c) and point symbol \((4^{2}{\cdot}8^{4})\) . See Figure S4 in the Supporting Information for details.[12]
The bulk phase purity of the sample was confirmed by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) (Figure S9, Supporting Information). Although the crystalline ordering of In-MOF is stable at ambient conditions for several days, the material loses its long-range ordering upon thermal treatment under dynamic vacuum as confirmed by X-ray studies. The thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) showed that up to \({\approx}160\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) solvent is desorbed and the remaining material is stable up to \(400\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figure S14, Supporting Information). Interestingly, the solvent-free less crystalline or even amorphous sample neither showed any significant change in thermal stability (as evidenced by TGA) nor changes of the characteristic UV–vis absorption and photoluminescence properties. Remarkably, there is also no visible change in morphology and transparency of the selected specimen ("single crystal") even if this specimen has lost crystallinity, as seen by X-ray diffraction (XRD) studies. To check the coordination between TCPE to \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\) and the shortrange ordering, the IR spectroscopic data of the as-synthesized and activated material in
comparison showed that there is no change or loss in coordination bonding between metal-ion nodes and TCPE linkers (Figure S16, Supporting Information).[13] Similar loss of long-range ordering and crystallinity in MOFs without breaking of the local or short-range framework connectivity upon solvent loss has been reported before and in most cases such behavior was considered as potential disadvantage.[13] However, in the case of the discussed optical properties, the effects caused by the loss of long-range order of the coordination network seem less significant. This observation may open up the opportunity to even think beyond crystallinity in MOFs and coordination polymers for optical functions.
The rigidification of highly fluorescent and otherwise conformationally quite flexible TCPE linkers between the metal nodes results in a great enhancement in the fluorescence properties with emission peaks at \(494~\mathrm{nm}\) upon excitation at \(400~\mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 2b and Figure S8, Supporting Information, ligand-based fluorescence due to the optically innocent behavior of \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\)), as compared to the parent, free acid \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{TCPE}\) with emission at \(534~\mathrm{nm}\). Motivated by this observation of strong fluorescence behavior, the single crystals of In-MOF were subjected to a 100 fs laser at \(800~\mathrm{nm}\). This laser was connected to an optical parametric amplifier (OPA) and coupled to a spectrometer microscope in order to observe the anticipated NLO behavior. Remarkably, the material showed very strong 2PEF with the emission maximum at \(487~\mathrm{nm}\) at \(\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}\,{=}\,800\,\mathrm{nm}\) (Figure S8, Supporting Information). The 2PEF peak is slightly blue shifted by \(7~\mathrm{nm}\) compared to the 1PEF \((494~\mathrm{nm})\) due to reabsorption and other mechanisms.[5d,14] The 2PEF has been quantified by comparing the emission data with a perylene reference sample at the same laser wavelength and power. The two-photon action cross-section value of 3072 GM found for In-MOF is drastically higher than the \(55~\mathrm{GM}\) of the parent \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{TCPE}\) (Figure 3a and Figure S5, Supporting Information).[15] The slope of the log–log dependence of the fluorescence intensity upon irradiation was found to be \(\approx2\) (power-dependent plots, Figure S12 in the Supporting Information), which confirms the third-order two-photon upconversion in the material. Wavelength-dependent 2PEF was measured to check for possible higher order nonlinearity. However, the compound did not show any higher-order nonlinear behavior, which might be due to forbidden selection rules or very low activity at larger wavelengths and higher nonlinear orders.[16] Upon the loss of crystallinity during solvent desorption, the 2PEF cross-section values reduced to 1111 GM, still a high value. In contrast to basic photophysical properties, which are mainly depending on the local structure, the solid state 2PEF property appears to be more depended on the proximate arrangement and the medium-range order of the linkers.[11c,d]
During the power-dependent measurements at \(400~\mathrm{nm}\) excitation, a prominent sharp peak evolved from the fluorescence peak which emphasizes the possible stimulated emission from In-MOF (Figure 2). By analysis of the fluorescence spectra (1PEF), we realized that the fluorescence peak can be deconvoluted into two Gaussian peaks (Figure S6, Supporting Information) with maxima at 482 and \(522~\mathrm{nm}\) and a full-width at half maximum (FWHM) of 84 and \(50~\mathrm{nm}\), respectively (Figure S6a,b, Supporting Information). Upon increasing the power of the laser, a sharp peak at \(472~\mathrm{nm}\) with a FWHM of \(18\pm2~\mathrm{nm}\) evolved from the broad 1PEF \((\mathrm{FWHM}=90~\mathrm{nm})\)
fluorescence spectrum (Figure S6c, Supporting Information). By plotting the power of the laser-dependent maximum intensity of the luminescence spectrum, the STE threshold was determined as \(36~\mathrm{mJ}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) (Figure 2b), which is comparable to reference materials in the literature and to commercial compounds (Table S1, Supporting Information). To further confirm the STE, time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) measurements were performed on these single crystals with laser powers below and above the STE threshold, which showed a decrease in fluorescence lifetime from 900 to \(500~\mathrm{ns}\), suggesting a faster recombination of exciton driven STE, which is consistent with the signature of stimulated emission as shown in Figure 2c.[3c,5b,c] This slow decay is comparable to many STE materials (Table S2, Supporting Information).[17]
A parallel or stacked arrangement of linkers should favor strong nonlinear 2PEF and spontaneous emission.[11c,d] The still comparably low-density packing of the linkers could therefore be the reason for the relatively small STE peak of In-MOF (density \(=0.72~\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{dm}^{-3}\)). Hence, design and synthesis of related MOFs with denser packing of TCPE was attempted for better
population inversion and STE. In particular, \( Z\mathrm{n}^{2+} \) was chosen as the mode due to its relatively low coordination number (from 4 to 6), absence of d–d transitions as in the case on \( \mathrm{In}^{3+} \) (which otherwise might interfere with the linker-based optical properties), and its environmentally friendly behavior different from \( \mathrm{In}^{3+} \). Yellow block-shaped single crystals of (DMA)[Zn(HTCPE)] (Zn-MOF; HTCPE = monoprotonated TCPE linker) were obtained by solvothermal synthesis employing \( \mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2} \cdot 4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \), \( \mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{TCPE} \), and \( 4,4^{\prime} \)-bipyridylethylene in DMF/water/1 M HCl solution at \( 110\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \). This Zn-MOF crystallized in the monoclinic space group \( P2_{1}/c \) and the structure was determined via single-crystal X-ray diffraction. \( Z\mathrm{n}^{2+} \) is coordinated to four carboxylates of TCPE in a distorted square pyramidal geometry. One carboxylate is coordinated in chelated manner, while the other three carboxylate groups are coordinated to \( Z\mathrm{n}^{2+} \) in monodentate fashion. One of the three monodentate carboxylates is protonated and the corresponding OH group is hydrogen bonded to two neighboring oxygen atoms of TCPE ligands. Overall, the \( Z\mathrm{n}^{2+} \)-centers act as four connecting nodes by coordinating to four TCPE linkers. This forms a fourfold interpenetrated 3D anionic framework (Figure 1 and Figure S3a, Supporting Information, linker packing density \( =1.5\ \mathrm{mol\cdotdm}^{-3} \)). One charge balancing DMA cation per formula unit is hosted in the narrow pores of the network, leaving no accessible porosity. If the TCPE linkers are again considered as double tricon
nected moieties, the topology of the framework is deduced as another rare dmd net with stoichiometry \( (3-\mathrm{c})_{2}(4-\mathrm{c}) \) and point symbol \( (4-10^{2})_{2}(4^{2}.10^{4}) \) with fourfold interpenetration. By further simplifying TCPE to four connected nodes, the topology of the framework is assigned as pts net with stoichiometry (4-c) (4-c) and point symbol \( (4^{2}.8^{4}) \) (Figure S4, Supporting Information).[12] Phase purity of Zn-MOF has also been confirmed by PXRD and a stability up to \( 400\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) was established by TGA (Figure S10 and S15, Supporting Information).
Nonlinear optical-absorption measurements were performed on Zn-MOF similar to In-MOF, which showed strong photoupconversion with a 2PEF action cross-section value of \( 1053\,\mathrm{GM} \) at \( 650\ \mathrm{nm} \) (Figure 3). The 2PEF has again been confirmed by power-dependent plots with slope \( \approx2 \) (Figure S12, Supporting Information). The stronger 2PEF behavior of In-MOF could be due to the different packing mode and conformation of the TCPE linkers.[11c,d] In contrast to Zn-MOF, the TCPE linkers of In-MOF turn out to be very nicely arranged (superimposable position) along the \( b \)-axis (Figure 1a), which may cause the higher 2PEF action cross-section values (Figure S3, Supporting Information).[11c,d] Power-dependent emission measurements have also been performed for Zn-MOF and remarkably better STE was found for Zn-MOF than for In-MOF with a sharp peak
at \( 473\ \mathrm{nm} \) \( (\mathrm{FWHM}=19\pm1\ \mathrm{nm}) \) away from the broad 1PEF spectrum \( (\mathrm{FWHM}=70\ \mathrm{nm}) \) with even lower threshold input laser fluence of \( 27\ \mathrm{mJ\ cm}^{-2} \) (Figure 4). The TRPL data show a decrease from \( 1.2\ \upmu\mathrm{s} \) to \( 380\ \mathrm{ns} \), which emphasizes the stimulated emission behavior of Zn-MOF (Figure 4c).[3c,5b,c] Interestingly, and unlike quantum dots, the STE peak is observed in the high energy region (blue side) of emission.[5b,c]
To gain a first insight and a quantitative understanding of the underlying mechanism of such a unique behavior, we performed electronic structure simulations based on density functional theory (DFT). For the electronic structure calculations, we used the all-electron code FHI-atms[18] and the dispersion-corrected generalized gradient functional PBE+vdW.[19] In our theoretical discussion, we focus on the Zn-MOF as this shows an enhanced and low threshold STE. As a starting structure for geometry optimization, we used the single-crystal X-ray diffraction data from above including four DMA cations per unit cell. The transition dipole moment governs the light-matter interaction strength between two states \( (\phi_{i},\ \phi_{j}) \) within the dipole approximation.[20] We calculated the transition dipole moments \( (\mu_{ij}) \) at the \( \Gamma \)-point between the occupied and unoccupied states \( \mu_{ij}=e|\langle\ \phi_{i}\ |\ r\ |\ \phi_{j}\rangle|^{[21]} \) for the energy range shown in the density of states (DOS) (Figure 5).
The transition dipole moments are colored according to their strength, the eight most dominant \(\mu_{ij}\) are marked in Figure 5b (see Table S1 in the Supporting Information). In our calculations, we identified four doubly degenerate transitions (Figure 5c, \((\mu_{1,12},\,\mu_{2,11})\); \((\mu_{1,14},\,\mu_{2,13})\); \((\mu_{3,10},\,\mu_{4,9})\); \((\mu_{3,15},\,\mu_{4,16})\)). The level diagram in Figure 5c schematically shows the transitions between the valence and conduction band at the \(\Gamma\)-point. Energy levels of the most dominant transitions between the conduction band to valence band are indicated by double sided arrows. At the employed semilocal level of theory, the band gap of \(E_{\mathrm{g}}=2.38\,\mathrm{eV}\) is severely underestimated, which translates to a general underestimation of absolute transition energies. However, the \(\mu_{ij}\) provides a good estimate of the transition strength between two states and gives a qualitative picture of the underlying physics. The transition dipole moments \((\mu_{1,12},\,\mu_{1,14},\,\mu_{2,13})\) between the valence band maximum (states 1, 2) and the excited states 12–14 in the conduction band show the highest transition dipole moments with \(\mu_{2,13}=0.90\,\mathrm{eA}\), \(\mu_{1,12}=0.88\,\mathrm{eA}\), and \(\mu_{1,14}=0.86\,\mathrm{eA}\) (see Table S3 in the Supporting Information). Most likely, \((\mu_{3,15},\,\mu_{4,16})\) is due to photoexcitation of the material and all other three transitions together cause the broad fluorescence spectra at lower excitation energies. Looking at the combined transition dipole moments \((\Sigma\mu)\) of degenerated transitions from Table S3 in the Supporting Information, we found that the most prominent transition is most likely \((\mu_{1,14},\,\mu_{2,13})\) with \(\Sigma\mu=1.76\,\mathrm{eA}\). The second highest transition has a \(7\%\) lower \(\Sigma\mu\). Thus, upon excitation of Zn-MOF at \(400\,\mathrm{nm}\) laser, the electrons transition from the ground state \((\phi_{3}\) and \(\phi_{4})\) to the excited state \((\phi_{15}\) and \(\phi_{16})\). Through radiationless decay, the electrons presumably move to the conduction band levels, \(\phi_{9}-\phi_{14}\). As the transition moment between the involved excited state is the highest for \(\mu_{15,13},\,\mu_{16,14}\), the spontaneous radiationless emission populates \(\phi_{13}\) and \(\phi_{14}\) at higher power of the laser and leads to a population inversion of these states. Hence, the STE is observed from these \(\phi_{13}\) and \(\phi_{14}\) levels. Due to the relative position of energy levels \(\phi_{13}\) and \(\phi_{14}\) (higher energy levels among the fluorescence transition levels), the STE
is observed at the blue region (higher energy region) of fluorescence. We also calculated the projected DOS (Figure S19, Supporting Information), projected on to the Zn-metal nodes, the TCPE linker molecules, and the DMA cations. Figure S19 (Supporting Information) shows that the occupied states \((\phi_{1}-\phi_{4})\) and the unoccupied states \((\phi_{9}-\phi_{16})\) are localized at the TCPE linker molecules. Further analysis of the eigenstate density (Figure S20, Supporting Information), shows a general trend of the eigenstate densities to be more localized at the center of the TCPE linker molecule for the occupied states and further outside for the unoccupied states. This possibly leads to a spatial separation of the electron hole pair and thereby reduces the probability of exciton recombination. We conclude that the transition dipole moments and the spatial charge separation between the occupied and unoccupied states enhance the STE behavior of the MOFs investigated in this work.
Similar to recent reports on dye loading of MOFs, attempts have been made to exchange the counter cations of In-MOF and Zn-MOF with the well-known cationic red dye molecule 4-[p-(dimethylamino)styryl]-1-methylpyridinium (DSMP) to probe a modulation of the intrinsic STE property.[5d–f] The exchange of the DMA cations in In-MOF was successful (Figure S19, Supporting Information), but failed for Zn-MOF. This was not unexpected because of the negligible porosity and higher density of linkers in Zn-MOF \((1.5\,\mathrm{mol\,dm}^{-3})\) compared to porous In-MOF \((0.72\,\mathrm{mol\,dm}^{-3})\). In particular, we were aiming to lower the STE threshold of In-MOF by DSMP inclusion. A proper arrangement of DSMP molecules within the framework would enhance the polarization in the material and thus could positively affect STE.[5d–f] However, DSMP guests showed significant fluorescence quenching and caused loss of NLO and STE properties. This behavior may evolve from the matching of UV–vis absorption and fluorescent emission bands of the dye and the MOF. Moreover, DSMP loaded In-MOF showed significant photobleaching under the laser (Figure S18, Supporting Information), whereas the parent In-MOF and Zn-MOF are very stable under laser illumination,
which was confirmed by exposing them to optical pulses of \(39\,\mathrm{mJ\,cm}^{-2}\) (Figure 3b). From these results, we conclude that temperature or other external stimuli-dependent (electric field) order/disorder effects of the orientation of optically innocent cations such as DMA in Zn-MOF or related densely packed TCPE-based anionic coordination networks should have some impact on polarization in the framework and consequently affect the photophysical behavior.[5f,22] However, these studies have been beyond the scope of this work.
In conclusion, we have synthesized and characterized two new anionic blue phosphor 3D interpenetrated MOFs with rare thi and dmd connectivity. These materials showed unique intrinsic stimulated emission with low fluence thresholds of 36 and \(27\,\mathrm{mJ\,cm}^{-2}\) for In- and Zn-MOFs, without any dye loading or doping and in the absence of external cavity. This intrinsic STE property depends on the packing density and on the arrangement of the TCPE linker molecules. The lower threshold powers of the Zn-MOF are assigned to the denser packing of linkers in the material. Exceptionally high nonlinear absorption with 2PEF action cross-section values up to 3072 GM were found for In-MOF, the highest for any hybrid solid state material so far. Our results not only reveal unique optical properties, but also high photo- and thermal-stability of these materials. These properties are assigned to the conformational rigidity and packing of the organic linkers that are pinned between the metal-ion nodes of the framework. Hence, we envision that these studies will open-up coordination network materials, in particular MOFs, as a platform of solid-state dielectrics for producing novel nonlinear and optical gain materials, thereby offering the potential of surpassing conventional dyes in solution/liquid/fluid systems with limited practical applicability.
## Experimental Section
Synthesis of In-MOF: A mixture of \(\mathsf{I n C l}_{3}\) (4 mg, 0.018 mmol) and \(\mathsf{H}_{4}\mathsf{T C P E}\) (8 mg, 0.009 mmol) was dissolved in DMF \((2\,\textrm{m L})\) , ethanol \((0.5\,\mathrm{~mL})\) , and \(10~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of 1 mM HCl in a \(4\,\textrm{m L}\) glass vial. This reaction mixture was placed in a programmable oven and heated at \(85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(48\,\mathrm{~h~}\) followed by cooling to room temperature at the rate of \(5\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\) . Pale-yellow block-shaped single crystals of In-MOF, (DMA)[In(TCPE)] (solv) were obtained and these crystals were washed with excess DMF and ethanol and dried at room temperature. Yield: 8 mg, \(73\%\) , Anal. Calcd. \((\%)\) : C, 69.36; H, 4.16; N, 3.44. Found: C, 69.65; H, 3.96; N, 1.60 (porous crystalline materials, MOFs are known to trap guest molecules; hence, it is difficult to fit the elemental analysis (EA) data. Thus, the calculated elemental content is provided based on the chemical formula of the solvent free sample (DMA)[In(TCPE)]).
Synthesis of Zn-MOF: A mixture of \(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) (14 mg, 0.054 mmol), \(\mathsf{H}_{4}\mathsf{T C P E}\) (8 mg, 0.009 mmol), and \(^{4,4^{\prime}}\) -bipyridylethene \((3\mathrm{~mg},\,0.017\mathrm{~mmol})\) were dissolved in DMF \((2\ m L)\) , methanol \((0.5\mathrm{~mL})\) , and \(10~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of 1 mM HCl in a \(4\,\textrm{m L}\) glass vial and placed in programmable oven and heated to \(110\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(48\,\mathrm{~h~}\) followed by cooling to room temperature at the rate of \(5\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\) . Pale-yellow rod-like single crystals of Zn-MOF, (DMA)[Zn(HTCPE)] were obtained. These crystals were washed with excess DMF and methanol, and dried at room temperature. Yield: \(7.5\ \mathrm{mg}\) , \(81\%\) . Anal. Calcd. \((\%)\) : C, 73.00; H, 4.49; N, 1.52. Found: C, 72.56; H, 4.25; N, 1.65.
Dye Loading in In-MOF: The In-MOF single crystals were placed in dil. DSMP solution in DMF and the solvent was exchanged every \(8\ \mathrm{~h~}\) with a slight increment of the dye concentration from \(0.25~\times\) \(10^{-3}\) to \(12\times10^{-3}\) M for 4 d. The NMR analysis of this sample (after
digestion) showed quantitative exchange of the DMA cation with DSMP (Figure S18, Supporting Information).
Two-Photon Excited PL and STE: 2PEF and STE of MOF crystals were measured with a 100 fs laser pulses (580–1000 nm wavelength and 1 kHz repetition rate) emitted from a mode-locked Ti:sapphire laser (Spectra Physics, Maitai-Spitfire) pumped OPA. The single-crystal MOF samples were kept on a glass plate; this glass plate was mounted on the microscope stage. The laser pulses were focused onto the sample with a \(10\times\) microscope objective (numerical aperture (NA) \(=0.3\) ). Opticaldensity filters were used to change the incident laser energy. The PL signal was collected using the same microscope objective in reflection mode and dispersed by a monochromator (Princeton Instruments, Isoplane) and the spectrum was recorded by a charged coupled device (Andor). During the measurement, a dichroic filter was used to block the pump beam. Transient PL signals were collected using fast photodiode (rise time \(\approx1\) ns), signals were coupled into the digital oscilloscope.
DFT Calculations: The density of states and transition dipole moments were obtained by numerically and basis-set converged DFT calculations using the FHI-aims all-electron code together with the dispersion-corrected generalized-gradient PBE+vdW functional. Brillouin-zone sampling was done on a \(6\times6\times2\) integration grid. As input geometries, the crystallographic data for Zn-MOFs were used including four DMA per unit cell and relaxed the atomic positions until forces were below \(0.02\,{\mathsf{e V}}\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1}\) .
